/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain WATER

  /* Two player islands on opposite sides of a Normal map, far enough apart
     that the connection path between them crosses open water nowhere near
     either island's own (already-beached) coastline. */
  create_player_lands
  {
    terrain_type GRASS
    land_percent 8
    base_size 8
    border_fuzziness 0
    circle_radius 80 0
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

  /* The only water-to-land conversion on the map. DIRT appears nowhere
     else, so every DIRT tile - and any beach found beside one - can only
     have come from this command. */
  create_connect_all_players_land
  {
    replace_terrain WATER DIRT
  }

/*
RMSTEST_70 - is the new land/water boundary a CONNECTION command carves
through open water dressed with beach, or does it ship bare?
Feeds: preview-design Sec.15 item 31, src/preview/generator/index.ts:281-289
(applyConnections' "DELIBERATELY no beach pass here" comment)

THE QUESTION, AND IT IS NARROW
DE paints its own beach at a land/water boundary with no command asking for
it. That step is pinned to run twice: at the end of land generation, and
again at the end of every create_terrain command (RMSTEST_58, Sec.15 item
28, CLOSED). Both of those moments are over by the time
<CONNECTION_GENERATION> runs, and the shipped preview generator deliberately
adds no third pass there, on the reasoning that inventing an unobserved
moment is worse than leaving a real gap - Pag alone carries 873 unbeached
coastal tiles as a result (Sec.6.4).

What is open is whether that reasoning is right. A create_connect_all_lands
family command with replace_terrain can convert WATER to a land terrain
along its path, which is a land/water boundary the engine has never dressed
before - it did not exist at the end of S4. Two readings:

  A THIRD PASS EXISTS -> the new DIRT/water boundary gets a beach ring, the
                          same way S1's and S4's boundaries do. The shipped
                          "no pass" comment is wrong and index.ts needs one.
  NO THIRD PASS        -> the new boundary ships bare, matching the current
                          code exactly. Sec.15 item 31 closes with no
                          change.

METHOD
An all-water map with two small player islands on opposite sides, connected
by one create_connect_all_players_land command carrying replace_terrain
WATER DIRT. That command is the ONLY thing on the map that ever puts a land
terrain next to water outside the two islands' own coastlines, and DIRT is
not used anywhere else, so every DIRT tile is attributable to it by
construction.

Read the DIRT path's own patch geometry first, then check for BEACH beside
its INTERIOR - the stretch of the path that runs through open water, away
from either island. The two islands' own coastlines will show ordinary
beach from the S1 pass; that is expected and not the question. The question
is only about beach touching DIRT tiles that sit in what was open water at
the end of S4.

  BEACH beside interior DIRT tiles -> a pass runs after S5.
  NO BEACH beside interior DIRT tiles, only at the path's two ends where it
    meets the islands' pre-existing beach rings -> no pass runs after S5,
    exactly as shipped.

WHY THE MAP IS BUILT THIS WAY
No <TERRAIN_GENERATION> commands at all, so item 28's already-closed
question (the per-command step's own scope) cannot contribute a confound.
No forest, leaves or shallows, which auto-place objects or complicate the
depth rule. circle_radius 80 0 puts the two islands close to opposite edges
of a Normal map so the path's middle third sits well clear of both
coastlines, giving the interior/end distinction a clean geometric readout
rather than a guess.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), 2 PLAYERS. TWO RUNS.

Two rather than one because a beach could in principle appear only where
the path reconnects with existing land and not along the open-water
interior, which a single generation cannot separate from noise in exactly
where the islands' own beach rings end.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches DIRT
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches BEACH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches GRASS
  Get the DIRT path's bounding box and compare it against the BEACH
  patches' bounding boxes. Beach overlapping the path's interior, away from
  both GRASS islands, is the third-pass reading; beach confined to the two
  ends is the shipped reading.
*/